1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,757
There is a prison in Colorado where the window is about four inches wide.

2
00:00:04,757 --> 00:00:07,038
It is built so you can see the sky,

3
00:00:07,038 --> 00:00:08,472
but not where you are.

4
00:00:08,472 --> 00:00:13,360
Most people in the American federal system will never get anywhere near it.

5
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:17,401
To reach it, you have to fall through every other level first.

6
00:00:17,401 --> 00:00:19,812
Today we climb down the whole ladder,

7
00:00:19,812 --> 00:00:22,875
one rung at a time, all the way to that window.

8
00:00:23,475 --> 00:00:24,304
Good evening.

9
00:00:24,304 --> 00:00:25,453
I'm the Archivero.

10
00:00:25,453 --> 00:00:27,622
I keep the files nobody asked for,

11
00:00:27,622 --> 00:00:30,939
in a room nobody visits, by the light of a lamp that

12
00:00:30,939 --> 00:00:32,278
never quite goes out.

13
00:00:32,278 --> 00:00:36,489
Tonight's file is a thick one: every security level of the federal

14
00:00:36,489 --> 00:00:38,722
prison system in the United States.

15
00:00:38,722 --> 00:00:44,399
Roughly a hundred and fifty-five thousand people live somewhere on this ladder right now.

16
00:00:44,399 --> 00:00:47,908
Some of them can walk out the front door every morning.

17
00:00:47,908 --> 00:00:51,735
Some of them have not seen another prisoner's face in years.

18
00:00:51,735 --> 00:00:54,989
The difference between those two lives is not luck.

19
00:00:54,989 --> 00:00:55,946
It is a number.

20
00:00:55,946 --> 00:00:58,051
So before we open the first door,

21
00:00:58,051 --> 00:01:00,475
let's look at how that number is made.

22
00:01:01,075 --> 00:01:03,960
When someone is sentenced to federal prison,

23
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:08,551
the Bureau of Prisons does not simply pick a building with a free bed.

24
00:01:08,551 --> 00:01:10,321
It fills in a scoring form.

25
00:01:10,321 --> 00:01:12,748
Points for how serious the crime was.

26
00:01:12,748 --> 00:01:15,043
Points for any history of violence.

27
00:01:15,043 --> 00:01:17,928
Points for any past escape, even an old one.

28
00:01:17,928 --> 00:01:20,813
Points for how long is left on the sentence,

29
00:01:20,813 --> 00:01:23,895
and for any other charges waiting in the wings.

30
00:01:23,895 --> 00:01:26,650
Add them up, and you get a security level.

31
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:31,109
Then staff can adjust it with what they call management variables: a

32
00:01:31,109 --> 00:01:35,306
gang conflict, a medical need, a request to be closer to family.

33
00:01:35,306 --> 00:01:37,339
The score is not fixed, either.

34
00:01:37,339 --> 00:01:40,224
Behave for years, and the points drift down.

35
00:01:40,224 --> 00:01:42,126
Start fights, and they climb.

36
00:01:42,126 --> 00:01:45,536
In other words, the ladder moves in both directions,

37
00:01:45,536 --> 00:01:47,175
and every rung is earned.

38
00:01:47,775 --> 00:01:50,167
We start at the bottom of the ladder,

39
00:01:50,167 --> 00:01:51,784
which is barely a ladder.

40
00:01:51,784 --> 00:01:55,793
A Residential Reentry Center, better known as a halfway house.

41
00:01:55,793 --> 00:01:59,931
The Bureau of Prisons contracts them to help people near the end

42
00:01:59,931 --> 00:02:02,259
of a sentence rebuild a normal life.

43
00:02:02,259 --> 00:02:05,039
Residents go out to work, look for housing,

44
00:02:05,039 --> 00:02:08,854
reconnect with family, then come back at night and sign in.

45
00:02:08,854 --> 00:02:09,436
No cells.

46
00:02:09,436 --> 00:02:09,953
No bars.

47
00:02:09,953 --> 00:02:14,026
Just a curfew, drug tests, and a staff member who knows exactly

48
00:02:14,026 --> 00:02:16,419
what time you said you would be back.

49
00:02:16,419 --> 00:02:20,104
Some people finish the very last stretch at home instead,

50
00:02:20,104 --> 00:02:25,018
on home confinement, with phone check-ins and the occasional surprise visit.

51
00:02:25,018 --> 00:02:29,545
It is the only kind of prison where the hardest part is the paperwork.

52
00:02:29,545 --> 00:02:33,230
Break the rules, though, and the ladder works in reverse.

53
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:34,200
You go back up.

54
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:36,701
Next, the federal prison camp.

55
00:02:36,701 --> 00:02:37,778
Minimum security.

56
00:02:37,778 --> 00:02:41,073
Dormitory housing, a relatively low number of staff,

57
00:02:41,073 --> 00:02:43,354
and limited or no perimeter fencing.

58
00:02:43,354 --> 00:02:46,712
Yes, at some of them you could technically walk away.

59
00:02:46,712 --> 00:02:50,831
People mostly don't, because walking away is a new federal crime,

60
00:02:50,831 --> 00:02:53,936
it adds years, and it sends you several rungs up.

61
00:02:53,936 --> 00:02:56,344
Camps are built around work: kitchens,

62
00:02:56,344 --> 00:03:01,096
groundskeeping, maintenance, sometimes jobs at the bigger prison next door.

63
00:03:01,096 --> 00:03:04,771
Many camps sit right beside a higher-security institution,

64
00:03:04,771 --> 00:03:07,749
like a small cottage in the shadow of a castle.

65
00:03:07,749 --> 00:03:11,995
The press likes to call them "Club Fed." When Martha Stewart served

66
00:03:11,995 --> 00:03:14,339
her sentence at the camp in Alderson,

67
00:03:14,339 --> 00:03:18,901
West Virginia, tabloids nicknamed it "Camp Cupcake." It is not a resort.

68
00:03:18,901 --> 00:03:22,260
There are counts, searches, and rules for everything.

69
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:26,125
It is just the level where the walls are mostly in your head.

70
00:03:26,725 --> 00:03:28,511
One step up: low security.

71
00:03:28,511 --> 00:03:31,738
Here the fence appears, and it brings a friend.

72
00:03:31,738 --> 00:03:36,133
Low-security federal institutions have double-fenced perimeters.

73
00:03:36,133 --> 00:03:39,567
Most people still live in dormitories or cubicles,

74
00:03:39,567 --> 00:03:42,177
rows of bunks separated by half walls,

75
00:03:42,177 --> 00:03:43,756
so privacy is a rumour.

76
00:03:43,756 --> 00:03:47,190
There are more officers per inmate than at a camp,

77
00:03:47,190 --> 00:03:49,868
and the day is organised around counts,

78
00:03:49,868 --> 00:03:52,684
work assignments, and education programs.

79
00:03:52,684 --> 00:03:57,903
Many people here were convicted of non-violent crimes but have sentences too

80
00:03:57,903 --> 00:04:01,131
long, or histories too complicated, for a camp.

81
00:04:01,131 --> 00:04:06,625
Think of it as the first level where the building is actively watching you back.

82
00:04:07,225 --> 00:04:09,448
Quick question before we go deeper.

83
00:04:09,448 --> 00:04:12,433
Which level do you think holds the most people?

84
00:04:12,433 --> 00:04:14,529
Leave your guess in the comments.

85
00:04:14,529 --> 00:04:17,577
I'll give you the answer at the end of the file,

86
00:04:17,577 --> 00:04:20,118
and it is less dramatic than you'd hope,

87
00:04:20,118 --> 00:04:22,150
which is exactly why it matters.

88
00:04:22,750 --> 00:04:23,821
Medium security.

89
00:04:23,821 --> 00:04:29,379
The perimeter gets stronger, often double fences with electronic detection systems,

90
00:04:29,379 --> 00:04:32,460
so touching the wire is not a secret for long.

91
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:35,741
Housing changes too: mostly cells now, not dorms.

92
00:04:35,741 --> 00:04:37,482
That means a door, a bunk,

93
00:04:37,482 --> 00:04:40,428
a toilet, and a roommate you did not choose.

94
00:04:40,428 --> 00:04:44,982
Medium institutions run a wide range of work and treatment programs,

95
00:04:44,982 --> 00:04:48,129
from factory jobs to drug treatment to classes,

96
00:04:48,129 --> 00:04:50,272
but movement is more controlled.

97
00:04:50,272 --> 00:04:52,013
You go where you are told,

98
00:04:52,013 --> 00:04:55,696
when you are told, and the schedule is the real warden.

99
00:04:55,696 --> 00:05:00,316
Staff numbers rise again, and so do internal controls: more searches,

100
00:05:00,316 --> 00:05:04,602
more cameras, more counts, including in the middle of the night.

101
00:05:04,602 --> 00:05:06,812
This is the middle of the ladder,

102
00:05:06,812 --> 00:05:10,629
and for many people it is the place where a long sentence

103
00:05:10,629 --> 00:05:13,575
actually happens, year after identical year.

104
00:05:14,175 --> 00:05:16,417
It helps to picture one ordinary day.

105
00:05:16,417 --> 00:05:18,478
Wake-up comes early, then a count,

106
00:05:18,478 --> 00:05:22,175
where everyone must be exactly where they are supposed to be.

107
00:05:22,175 --> 00:05:22,781
Breakfast.

108
00:05:22,781 --> 00:05:24,962
Work call: the kitchen, the laundry,

109
00:05:24,962 --> 00:05:26,962
the factory floor, the classroom.

110
00:05:26,962 --> 00:05:27,811
Another count.

111
00:05:27,811 --> 00:05:28,174
Lunch.

112
00:05:28,174 --> 00:05:28,780
More work.

113
00:05:28,780 --> 00:05:30,720
Recreation, if the yard is open.

114
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:31,326
Mail call.

115
00:05:31,326 --> 00:05:31,750
Dinner.

116
00:05:31,750 --> 00:05:32,598
Another count.

117
00:05:32,598 --> 00:05:33,144
Lockdown.

118
00:05:33,144 --> 00:05:35,507
And then, while you sleep, more counts,

119
00:05:35,507 --> 00:05:39,568
a flashlight checking that the shape in the bed is still breathing.

120
00:05:39,568 --> 00:05:43,507
Change the level, and the shape of the day stays almost the same.

121
00:05:43,507 --> 00:05:47,022
What changes is the space around it: how far you can walk,

122
00:05:47,022 --> 00:05:51,325
how many doors you pass, and how many people are watching while you do.

123
00:05:51,925 --> 00:05:52,911
High security.

124
00:05:52,911 --> 00:05:58,193
In the federal system these are the United States Penitentiaries, the USPs.

125
00:05:58,193 --> 00:06:02,347
Highly secured perimeters, with walls or reinforced fences,

126
00:06:02,347 --> 00:06:04,883
and towers or patrols watching them.

127
00:06:04,883 --> 00:06:07,347
Single and multiple-occupant cells.

128
00:06:07,347 --> 00:06:11,573
The highest ratio of staff to inmates of the regular levels,

129
00:06:11,573 --> 00:06:15,094
and close control of every movement: when you eat,

130
00:06:15,094 --> 00:06:17,277
when you walk, when you shower.

131
00:06:17,277 --> 00:06:21,995
This is where the system sends people convicted of the most violent

132
00:06:21,995 --> 00:06:24,530
crimes, or with histories of escape,

133
00:06:24,530 --> 00:06:27,418
gang activity, or violence inside prison.

134
00:06:27,418 --> 00:06:30,657
The most famous penitentiary of all is closed.

135
00:06:30,657 --> 00:06:34,601
Alcatraz operated as a federal prison from 1934 to 1963,

136
00:06:34,601 --> 00:06:38,051
on a rock in the cold water of San Francisco Bay.

137
00:06:38,051 --> 00:06:43,192
Over those years, thirty-six men tried to escape it in fourteen attempts.

138
00:06:43,192 --> 00:06:44,389
Most were caught.

139
00:06:44,389 --> 00:06:45,446
Some were shot.

140
00:06:45,446 --> 00:06:46,291
Two drowned.

141
00:06:46,291 --> 00:06:48,051
Officially, none made it.

142
00:06:48,051 --> 00:06:52,699
But five were never found, including the three men who left behind

143
00:06:52,699 --> 00:06:57,417
papier-mâché heads in their beds one night in June 1962 and paddled

144
00:06:57,417 --> 00:07:00,375
into the dark on a raft made of raincoats.

145
00:07:00,975 --> 00:07:03,646
But there is a level inside every level.

146
00:07:03,646 --> 00:07:07,252
Most federal institutions have a Special Housing Unit,

147
00:07:07,252 --> 00:07:09,322
the SHU, or as inmates call it,

148
00:07:09,322 --> 00:07:12,994
"the hole." This is where people go for breaking rules,

149
00:07:12,994 --> 00:07:16,400
during investigations, or for their own protection.

150
00:07:16,400 --> 00:07:20,139
Time in the SHU usually means being locked in a cell for

151
00:07:20,139 --> 00:07:24,613
most of the day, with meals delivered through a slot and recreation

152
00:07:24,613 --> 00:07:25,681
in a small cage.

153
00:07:25,681 --> 00:07:28,953
It is not a separate prison you are sentenced to.

154
00:07:28,953 --> 00:07:31,491
It is a place the prison can send you,

155
00:07:31,491 --> 00:07:33,093
any day, from any level.

156
00:07:33,093 --> 00:07:37,300
Which is why, in practice, every security level has a basement.

157
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:40,028
And now, the bottom of the file.

158
00:07:40,028 --> 00:07:45,813
The Bureau of Prisons calls the last category "administrative." These institutions have

159
00:07:45,813 --> 00:07:48,872
special missions: holding people before trial,

160
00:07:48,872 --> 00:07:56,186
treating serious medical cases, or containing people considered extremely dangerous, violent, or escape-prone.

161
00:07:56,186 --> 00:07:59,179
The most extreme is ADX Florence in Colorado,

162
00:07:59,179 --> 00:08:03,767
opened in 1994 and nicknamed "the Alcatraz of the Rockies." Cells are

163
00:08:03,767 --> 00:08:07,092
roughly seven by twelve feet, with a concrete bed,

164
00:08:07,092 --> 00:08:10,283
a concrete desk, a concrete stool, and a shower.

165
00:08:10,283 --> 00:08:15,337
Most prisoners there spend about twenty-three hours a day alone in the cell.

166
00:08:15,337 --> 00:08:17,066
Food comes through a slot.

167
00:08:17,066 --> 00:08:21,920
Exercise happens alone, in an outdoor pen that some former prisoners have

168
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:24,248
compared to an empty swimming pool.

169
00:08:24,248 --> 00:08:26,974
And the window is about four inches wide,

170
00:08:26,974 --> 00:08:29,900
angled so you see sky but not the landscape,

171
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:33,158
so you cannot tell where in the building you are.

172
00:08:33,158 --> 00:08:37,879
Over the years, the people held there have included Zacarias Moussaoui,

173
00:08:37,879 --> 00:08:41,137
Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, and Joaquín "El Chapo" Guzmán.

174
00:08:41,137 --> 00:08:45,792
There is a step-down program, a slow climb through several phases that

175
00:08:45,792 --> 00:08:47,986
can, after years of good conduct,

176
00:08:47,986 --> 00:08:50,513
lead back to an ordinary penitentiary.

177
00:08:50,513 --> 00:08:53,639
At this level, the punishment is not the walls.

178
00:08:53,639 --> 00:08:55,700
It is the silence between them.

179
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:58,393
So, the answer to the question.

180
00:08:58,393 --> 00:09:03,727
The level holding the most people is not the supermax and not the penitentiary.

181
00:09:03,727 --> 00:09:05,009
It is low security.

182
00:09:05,009 --> 00:09:07,440
In recent Bureau of Prisons figures,

183
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:10,276
it holds more people than any other level,

184
00:09:10,276 --> 00:09:12,301
well over a third of everyone.

185
00:09:12,301 --> 00:09:14,866
The ladder is not a tower of monsters.

186
00:09:14,866 --> 00:09:17,297
It is mostly a long, crowded middle.

187
00:09:17,297 --> 00:09:20,740
And here is the strangest detail in the whole file.

188
00:09:20,740 --> 00:09:24,859
In Florence, Colorado, a single federal complex holds a camp,

189
00:09:24,859 --> 00:09:27,762
a medium, a penitentiary, and the supermax,

190
00:09:27,762 --> 00:09:30,530
side by side on the same stretch of road.

191
00:09:30,530 --> 00:09:34,716
The whole ladder, from walk-away fences to a four-inch window,

192
00:09:34,716 --> 00:09:36,606
within sight of one another.

193
00:09:36,606 --> 00:09:41,197
Next time, we open the file on the strangest punishments in history.

194
00:09:41,197 --> 00:09:42,750
I'll leave the lamp on.

